WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.390
In this very short video, I want to

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.870
mention some of the very common mistakes

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.790
I see students making in when they're

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.270
doing their VHDL.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.710
And so these are really the things that

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.350
commonly catch students out when they're

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.350
doing their designs. Now, in this

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.150
introductory course I focused on using

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.990
signals to try and avoid this first

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.030
error, which is forgetting

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.790
the difference between a signal and a

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.870
variable in VHDL. They have different

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.775
update semantics.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.630
So, when we inside a process. So, in a

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.030
process, a signal is updated when the

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.790
process is suspends. So, when we define

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.990
a signal S as I say a standard logic,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.390
um that will be updated on a process. If

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.190
we use a variable in a process,

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.190
then it might be updated immediately

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.990
inside the process, right? So, when we

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.950
we we assign a value to a variable, it's

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.150
updated immediately, but if we assign a

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.270
value to a signal, it's updated at the

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.110
end. So, they have different semantics.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.189
Um another common mistake is that

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:05.750
students will make is they use the wrong

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.430
kinds of signal assignments. So, one of

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.150
the reasons we have two types of signal

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.310
assignment in VHDL is to try and avoid

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.830
and make that first error. So, signal we

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.110
use this less than or equal um type

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.630
symbol for signal assignment. Uh this is

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.510
a simulation only example where we're

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.510
assigning the value D to Q after 1

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.230
nanosecond. So, this has got a delay in

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.150
it. Um so, clearly simulation only.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.190
That's a signal assignment. Variable

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.070
assignment we use the colon equal

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.990
symbol. Um as I said, this would cause V

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.750
to be updated immediately.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.310
If this was in a process, well, this

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:39.670
would uh

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.870
would update D after an an um an ano- um

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.750
1 nanosecond. So,

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.790
uh another common error that have in is

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.710
sensitivity list

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:50.910
errors, particularly in combinational

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.550
processes. S- Often, students will miss

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.110
or forget to mention the signals.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.070
So,

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.510
here I have a process

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.990
and I've listed two signals in the

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.350
sensitivity list A and B, and inside

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.070
this I've got an assignment which the Y

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.390
signal Y is assigned to A and B and C.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.611
Now,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.949
with this particular case, the

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:17.670
sensitivity list only mentions two of

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.230
these.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.910
So, C changes, this Y doesn't change,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:23.990
okay?

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.990
We would have to mention C here, or or

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.030
list no no no signals at all. So, this

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.310
is a pure combinational process.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.110
But, Y is only updated if A or B change.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.310
Now, the the result depends on all three

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.150
signals.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.630
But, only A and B will cause it to

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:40.990
change. That's another very common

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.741
error.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.070
Uh another one, and again a very common

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.990
one, is what's called inadvertent latch

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.949
inference. And this is in a process not

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:54.590
assigning outputs in all branches.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.470
So, here we have a process sensitive to

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.550
A and B, and we've got another

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.910
two signals, and we've got another

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.949
signal Y, and which is changing. Now,

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:06.270
here you'll notice I've written if A is

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.670
equal to 1,

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:08.270
uh

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.252
then Y is assigned the value 1 end if.

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:14.509
If A isn't equal to 1, if A is 0, we

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:17.509
don't assign a value to Y.

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:19.150
What's going to What has to happen is

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.190
well, that means that the circuit has to

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.390
remember a value of Y. Right? So, if A

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.870
if if A say was set to 0, it has to hold

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.990
the value somewhere. It has to infer a

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.190
latch.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.229
Now, typically if we use the AMD tools,

00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:35.990
if we use the Vivado tools, they should

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.630
give a warning about inferred latches.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.750
If you have a look inside the messages,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.350
you'll find a warning.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.670
This is typically not what you want, and

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.870
typically this will give rise to timing

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.949
issues in your circuit. So, when you're

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:51.710
doing conditional branches, make sure

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.590
that all signals that are assigned a

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.910
value are always assigned a value,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.470
right? No matter what happens inside it.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:03:59.950
So, how might we do that? We might have

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.230
an else statement, might so and then

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.590
else Y is assigned the other value, I

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:05.830
assume.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.870
That would do it. Another approach I

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.550
take quite often when we have say in

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.150
when doing state machines where I have a

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.150
long case statement and then I only I

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.470
only want to change one signal

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.870
depending on the particular state.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.229
I would actually assign

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:22.670
default values at the beginning of the

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.550
process for all of the signals.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.630
And then in in my design, that means

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:28.990
that those are the default values. Then

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.070
in my design later on down down in the

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.790
process, I would then assert the values

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.950
that I want to I actually wanted to

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.270
assert for.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:38.790
And at the end of the process, of

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.350
course, the actual value given to a

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.990
signal is the last assignment. So, if

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.790
it's not assigned one in in the

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.390
conditional statement, a case statement,

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.830
or an if statement, it's going to take

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.710
the default value I assigned at the

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.630
beginning.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.990
Another common mistake is in doing

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.270
synthesizable designs is

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.150
quite often students forget that some

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.870
kinds of things can't be done in synthesis.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.550
So, for example, you can't use the

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.190
timing things like after

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.590
statements. Those kind of statements are

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.790
are simulation only.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.390
You can't do real math

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.590
in your calculation using real numbers.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.270
If you want to use real numbers in your

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.150
hardware design, there is an IEEE

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.430
library and an IEEE type and then you're

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.950
actually designing it in hardware, but

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.550
you can't use the real math functions in

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.070
VHDL.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.350
You can't also use recursion and

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.150
recursive procedures.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:33.830
And

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.310
And finally, you can't use file IO in

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.830
the synthesized design. Now, you can use

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.030
it just just the caveat to that, you

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.150
could use it for example to initialize a

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.230
value that's used in the hardware. An

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.270
example might be for that you have let's

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.550
say a program and it's a bit file. It's

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.190
a set of bits that you want to load into

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.550
RAM in your final design. You can

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.710
actually set it up that that will be

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:01.830
read and used to initialize let's say a

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.070
vector of bytes and words.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.350
And so you can use file IO to do that.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:08.910
What you can't have is your hardware

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.270
trying to read a file.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.030
And the other thing is

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.030
common mistake is using standard logic

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.270
vector with arithmetic without using the

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.390
numeric standard and using signed and

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.190
unsigned. So again, you know, do use the

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.590
appropriate types, the numeric types. I

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.350
should also say that you should always

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.630
in in use standard logic vector and

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.510
standard logic and standard logic vector

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.190
in hardware designs

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.670
as those types rather than say bit

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.270
types, the inbuilt types in VHDL, as

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.390
these

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.670
map more closely to hard how hardware

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:44.390
works and will behave more appropriately

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.270
for hardware designs.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.909
So that was just a list of very common

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:51.590
mistakes that I wanted to give.

